:: HO Part 1
The early lives of <strong>Beverly "Bev" Lustig</strong> are lost to history, perhaps intentionally. She is believed to have been at least three centuries young when she emerged as a public figure, and that she had gone through several names and lifetimes by then.

Lustig established herself as an underground lifemod arist in the hallowed "Methone Scene," but quickly emerged as a mainstream and fashionable personal brand. Her cute/unnerving personalized familiars could be seen peeeking from the purses of influencers and hoi polloi alike and she amassed untold billions of credits.

[[She is generally considered one of the key figures in NaNth Century culture.|HO Part 2]]

:: HO Part 2
For such a high profile artist, trendsetter, and mogul, Lustig remained an enigma to all but the closest of confidants. She seldom granted interviews, and if she held any strong moral or political beliefs she kept them to herself.

Beyond appearing at the occasional milquetoast charity gala, an occasional ingénue on her arm, she remained secluded in her Lunar mansion, releasing a new model of organic fashion accessory each year. Like clockwork. To inevitable acclaim and financial success.

[[Then she stopped.|HO Part 3]]

:: HO Part 3
A year went by without a new release by Bev Lustig. Then a second and third. Her older models still outsold all the competitors and knockoffs, but there was general unease in the industry. Could the lifemod art world survive without a Lustigian singularity at its center?

Her massive wealth and access to the finest of life extension technology made a hidden illness unlikely. But what could it be?

[[Nobody knew.|HO Part 4]]

:: HO Part 4
[[The answer was far more prosaic.|HO Part 5]]

:: HO Part 5
Bev was bored.

